@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "PRIMARY KEY";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            PRIMARY KEY
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            A PRIMARY KEY is a constraint used to uniquely identify
            each record in a SQL Server table. A primary key does not
            allow duplicate values and does not allow NULL values.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a PRIMARY KEY?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A PRIMARY KEY is one of the most important constraints in
            SQL Server. It identifies each row in a table uniquely.
        </p>

        <p>
            A table can have only one PRIMARY KEY constraint, but the
            primary key can contain one column or multiple columns.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Example
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In a Students table, StudentId can be used as the
                PRIMARY KEY because every student should have a
                unique StudentId.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRIMARY KEY RULES -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            PRIMARY KEY Rules
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                A PRIMARY KEY uniquely identifies each record.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Duplicate values are not allowed.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                NULL values are not allowed.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                A table can have only one PRIMARY KEY constraint.
            </li>

            <li>
                A PRIMARY KEY can contain one or multiple columns.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- CREATE TABLE WITH PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Creating a PRIMARY KEY
        </h2>

        <p>
            A PRIMARY KEY can be defined while creating a table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            Here, <strong>StudentId</strong> is the PRIMARY KEY of the
            Students table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- OUTPUT -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Each StudentId is unique, so it can identify a student
            without ambiguity.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DUPLICATE VALUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Duplicate Values are Not Allowed
        </h2>

        <p>
            Because StudentId is a PRIMARY KEY, the same StudentId
            cannot be inserted twice.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (101, 'Amit', 24);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0">

            <strong>Error:</strong>
            The INSERT operation fails because StudentId 101 already
            exists as a PRIMARY KEY value.
            
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- NULL VALUE -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            NULL Values are Not Allowed
        </h2>

        <p>
            A PRIMARY KEY column cannot contain NULL values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES (NULL, 'Rahul', 22);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0">

            <strong>Error:</strong>
            A NULL value cannot be inserted into a PRIMARY KEY column.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- NAMED PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Named PRIMARY KEY Constraint
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can give a specific name to the PRIMARY KEY constraint.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Age INT,

    CONSTRAINT PK_Students
    PRIMARY KEY (StudentId)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ADD PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Add PRIMARY KEY to Existing Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            If a table already exists, a PRIMARY KEY can be added
            using ALTER TABLE.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>ALTER TABLE Students
ADD CONSTRAINT PK_Students
PRIMARY KEY (StudentId);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMPOSITE PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Composite PRIMARY KEY
        </h2>

        <p>
            A composite primary key uses two or more columns together
            to uniquely identify a record.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-3">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE StudentCourses
(
    StudentId INT,
    CourseId INT,

    CONSTRAINT PK_StudentCourses
    PRIMARY KEY (StudentId, CourseId)
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            In this example, the combination of StudentId and CourseId
            must be unique.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- PRIMARY KEY DIAGRAM -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            PRIMARY KEY Structure
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL Server
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                                                     Students Table
                                                          |
                                                          ↓
                                                      StudentId
                                                          |
                                                          ↓
                                                     PRIMARY KEY
                                                          |
                                                          ├── Unique
                                                          |
                                                          └── NOT NULL
                                                          |
                                                          ↓
                                               Uniquely Identifies Student</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- VERIFY PRIMARY KEY -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Verify PRIMARY KEY
        </h2>

        <p>
            The following query can be used to view the primary key
            information of a table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>EXEC sp_help 'Students';</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    PRIMARY KEY uniquely identifies a row.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Duplicate PRIMARY KEY values are not allowed.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    PRIMARY KEY cannot contain NULL values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Only one PRIMARY KEY constraint can exist per table.
                </li>

                <li>
                    A PRIMARY KEY can contain multiple columns.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned about PRIMARY KEY,
                its rules, how to create it, how to add it to an
                existing table and how composite primary keys work.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


